Phases 2 and 3 handle encoding, scaling, class rebalancing via SMOTE, and feature selection in a strict, deliberate sequence. First, one-hot encoding and MinMax scaling are fitted strictly on the training set to prevent test leakage. Next, a Pearson correlation filter drops near-duplicate features exceeding a set threshold (|r| > thr) as a lightweight pass that removes redundancy so recursive feature elimination does not waste capacity on collinear twins. Random Forest RFE then narrows the remaining features down to the top k most informative ones. After feature selection, SMOTE is applied exclusively to the training rows—never to the test set—with benign traffic first undersampled for speed, avoiding the inflated accuracy caused by oversampling before splitting or leaking synthetic test neighbors into training. Finally, a set of untouched benign rows (benign_ref) is preserved separately so the unsupervised Isolation Forest trains on genuine normal traffic rather than SMOTE-generated points.
